from silora Allah knows his way around  kitchens back in his hometown Hama in  Syria he had his own restaurant but  since he emigrated three years ago he  opened up a Syrian specialty restaurant  in downtown Istanbul he was lucky to be  able to start anew while in exile in  Turkey at first the Turkish neighbors  had reservations they let their feelings  be felt but at one point they accepted  the fact that we have to survive - then  we have a good relationship - most  people here business is good my cousins  and I own 14 shops in Istanbul the  restaurant is in a quarter that people  call little Damascus the three hundred  thousand Syrian refugees in Istanbul  have created an infrastructure for  themselves they own cafés bakeries  travel agencies and real estate  companies the Syrians here have settled  many of them want to stay in Turkey the  Turkish government rejects criticism of  its refugee policy they are pointing at  the improvement Syrian refugees in  Turkey nowadays get work permits and the  free healthcare  these young Syrians say that's only half  the truth for them there are still  enough reasons to leave Turkey and  continue on to Europe to try their luck  we can work here but the pain is  terrible and we're not insured lawyers  are not obliged to pay insurance for  Syrians medical procedures have to be  paid for only surgery is free and  state-run hospitals plus we have to pay  for medicine in Syria I had my own shop  but here I have to work as a waiter in a  cafe 15 hours a day and I get half of  what a Turk earns any there's no future  for me here I'm going to stick to my  goal of going to your agency after Mecca  and Sun that's why here in little  Damascus the life vests salesman will  continue to do good business with  migrants
 

